Declare the Objective-C compile actions

objc_library() checks cc_common.action_is_enabled("objc-compile") and
fails with "Compiling objc_library targets requires the Apple CC
toolchain" unless the toolchain declares that action.  unix_cc's config
never did, so any toolchain built on it -- including toolchains_llvm --
could not build Objective-C at all.

clang drives Objective-C with the same binary and the same flags as
C/C++; the language comes from the source extension.  So declare
objc-compile and objc++-compile with the same tool and the same implied
features as the C/C++ actions.

Getting the flags there takes two things.  Adding the actions to
all_compile_actions (and objc++ to all_cpp_compile_actions) covers every
feature keyed off those lists.  The rest of the features spell their
actions out literally, and simply omit objc; the one that matters most
is "sysroot", without which the compile cannot find so much as
<unistd.h>.  Mirror c-compile to objc-compile and c++-compile to
objc++-compile in those, so an Objective-C translation unit is compiled
the same way a C one is.  The C++ module and header-parsing features are
left alone deliberately.

